      "text": "To determine how many four-digit numbers \\( N = \\underline{a}\\,\\underline{b}\\,\\underline{c}\\,\\underline{d} \\) satisfy all the given conditions, we need to carefully analyze each condition step by step.\n\n1. **Condition for Range of \\( N \\):**\n   \\[\n   4000 \\le N < 6000\n   \\]\n   This means the first digit \\( a \\) must be either 4 or 5. So, \\( a \\) can independently be either 4 or 5.\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Choices for } a: \\quad 4, 5\n   \\]\n\n2. **Condition for Digit Multiplicity:**\n   \\[\n   N \\text{ is a multiple of 5.}\n   \\]\n   For a number to be a multiple of 5, the last digit \\( d \\) must be either 0 or 5. So, \\( d \\) can independently be 0 or 5.\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Choices for } d: \\quad 0, 5\n   \\]\n\n3. **Condition for B and C Values:**\n   \\[\n   3 \\le b < c \\le 6\n   \\]\n   This condition implies that \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) must satisfy:\n   \\[\n   3 \\le b < c \\le 6\n   \\]\n\nLet's enumerate the possible values for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) that meet this criterion:\n\n- If \\( b = 3 \\):\n  \\[\n  c \\in \\{4, 5, 6\\}\n  \\]\n\n  The possible pairs \\((b, c)\\) are:\n  \\[\n  (3, 4), (3, 5), (3, 6)\n  \\]\n\n- If \\( b = 4 \\):\n  \\[\n  c \\in \\{5, 6\\}\n  \\]\n\n  The possible pairs \\((b, c)\\) are:\n  \\[\n  (4, 5), (4, 6)\n  \\]\n\n- If \\( b = 5 \\):\n  \\[\n  c \\in \\{6\\}\n  \\]\n\n  The possible pair \\((b, c)\\) is:\n  \\[\n  (5, 6)\n  \\]\n\n- If \\( b = 6 \\):\n  \\[\n  c \\notin \\{6\\} \\quad \\text{(but this does not fit the criteria as \\( c \\le 6 \\))}\n  \\]\n\nSumming up all valid pairs for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\), we have:\n\\[\n(3, 4), (3, 5), (3, 6), (4, 5), (4, 6), (5, 6)\n\\]\n\nThus, there are 6 valid choices for \\((b, c)\\).\n\n4. **Choosing the Last Digit \\( d \\):**\n   For each valid pair \\((b, c)\\), \\( d \\) can be either 0 or 5.\n\n5. **Determining the First Digit \\( a \\):**\n   For each valid pair \\((b, c)\\), \\( a \\) can be either 4 or 5.\n\n6. **Calculating Total Combinations:**\n   - Since \\( a \\) has 2 choices (4 or 5),\n   - Since there are 6 valid pairs \\((b, c)\\),\n   - Since \\( d \\) has 2 choices (0 or 5),\n   - The total number of valid 4-digit numbers \\( N \\) is:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 6 \\times 2 = 24\n     \\]\n\nThe final number of four-digit numbers \\( N \\) that satisfy all the given conditions is \\(\\boxed{24}\\).",
